{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CHANGE 1336 IS UP: https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/1336 (commit 19ef26e0d4, NOT submitted, awaiting LLM-Review and Verified).\n\nRE-VERIFIED ON THE FRESHEST origin/main after the operator asked whether this was a stale-repo artifact. IT IS NOT. At origin/main 9af88c3ada (which has advanced twice since this ticket was filed): rebar.toml's sections are still only [verify], [ticket], [mcp], [jira], [identity], bedrock mentions 0, and a clean-env resolution in a worktree at that commit still returns frontier -> anthropic:claude-opus-4-8, standard -> anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6, trivial -> anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5. The gap is real and current.\n\nWHAT LANDS IN 1336. rebar.toml gains BOTH:\n    [llm]\n    model = \"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\"\n    [llm.model_classes]\n    frontier = { model = \"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\" }\n    standard = { model = \"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6\" }\n    trivial  = { model = \"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0\" }\nMeasured before/after in a clean env: the three classes flip from anthropic:* to the three bedrock: profiles, AND cfg.model flips from the bare literal 'claude-opus-4-8' to 'bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8'.\n\nA CORRECTION TO THIS TICKET'S OWN APPROACH TEXT, which was mine and was imprecise. It says 'Do NOT set [llm] model as a scalar; use [llm.model_classes]'. What I MEANT was: do not use the scalar INSTEAD OF the class table. Both are required, and setting only the classes would have left a real leak open: cfg.model is a SECOND resolution path the class table cannot reach, defaulting to the bare literal 'claude-opus-4-8' which infers provider anthropic — the exact ambient-default leak [rebar:f124-f14a-fd62-4973] closed after MEASURING three tests calling direct Anthropic on non-Anthropic arms. Setting the scalar alone would instead collapse the frontier/standard split ([rebar:eb58-e71d-ec90-4b00]). So: scalar as COMPANION, never as substitute. The implementer flagged the contradiction rather than silently following the wrong half, which is the correct behaviour; the fault was in my wording, not their reading.\n\nTESTS — 4 in tests/unit/test_project_default_provider.py, each mutation-verified RED with recorded output: the table removed (3 failed, including the cfg.model companion assertion); the rebar.toml id changed alone; the OVERLAY id changed alone (proving the pin is two-sided, not one); a class dropped (proving exact class-set equality); and the opt-out assertion inverted. Full offline suite 9808 passed / 42 skipped, exit 0, with ZERO existing tests modified.\n\nWHY NO EXISTING TEST BROKE, which is worth recording because it looked like a serious risk. tests/conftest.py has an autouse fixture _default_rebar_root_to_sandbox that points REBAR_ROOT at a session-scoped EMPTY sandbox repo for every test. So no test resolves config against the real checkout's rebar.toml, and the tests that assert anthropic defaults were always testing the BUILT-IN FALLBACK AGAINST AN EMPTY CONFIG ROOT — not the project default. That is the distinction that decides whether such a test is stale or still correct, and here they are still correct as written. The implementer hit it as a genuine failure first (cfg.model resolving to the bare literal inside pytest while resolving to bedrock from the shell), which is the evidence for the classification rather than an assumption.\n\nAC5 IS RESOLVED WITHOUT AN EPIC EDIT, and this closes the implementer's open question. AC5 asks that the epic-level claim the 'repo config / local env' switch is complete be corrected wherever it appears. I searched epic 061c's own description for 'repo config' / 'repo-config' / 'local env' / 'local dev': ZERO matching lines. The overstatement did not come from the epic or from any tracked file — it came from a git-ignored local handoff note (LOOP-PROMPT.local.md) written by a previous session. So there is nothing in the repo or the store to correct, and no epic description edit is warranted (which is also the safer outcome, since editing an epic's text can invalidate plan-review material pins on it and its children). AC5 is satisfied by this record.\n\nSTILL OWED BEFORE THE EPIC CAN CLOSE, per operator instruction: 1336 must MERGE, and LOCAL rebar use of Bedrock must then be CONFIRMED BY OBSERVATION — a real local LLM op whose per-call record shows bedrock: models. Configuring it is not the same as confirming it, and this ticket is not closed until the second half is evidenced."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785807686264555001,"uuid":"61f61f6e-ffb6-4020-9257-fe347f8b2613"}